---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import PresenceCursorDemo from "../../components/PresenceCursorDemo";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Presence Cursor"
  description="Other people's pointers with a name tag or an avatar."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Presence Cursor</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Another person's pointer on a shared surface, like a canvas or a document.
    It shows a colored arrow with a name tag, or with an avatar.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><PresenceCursorDemo client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Presence Cursor usage"
      code={`import { PresenceCursor } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/presence-cursor";
import { Avatar, AvatarFallback, AvatarImage } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/avatar";

<div className="relative">
  {peers.map(peer => (
    <PresenceCursor
      key={peer.id}
      name={peer.email}
      color={peer.color}
      x={peer.x}
      y={peer.y}
    />
  ))}
</div>

<PresenceCursor
  name="jenny@gmail.com"
  color="#b55309"
  x={120}
  y={80}
  avatar={
    <Avatar size="sm">
      <AvatarImage src={jenny.image} alt="" />
      <AvatarFallback>JE</AvatarFallback>
    </Avatar>
  }
/>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> place the arrow tip, in px, from the top left
      of the nearest positioned parent. Give that parent <code
        >position: relative</code
      >. Your app sends the positions, for example from a realtime channel. The
      cursor does not track anything by itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>color</code> fills the arrow, the tag, and the avatar ring. The tag text
      is white, so pick colors with at least 4.5:1 contrast against white. Pass
      <code>avatar</code> to show it in place of the tag. A small Avatar fits best.
      Long names cut off with an ellipsis at 16rem.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      Each cursor is an image named after the person, so screen readers read the
      name in both forms. It ignores pointer events, so clicks go through it. It
      glides to a new spot over 180ms, and jumps when reduced motion is
      requested. In forced colors mode the arrow uses the system text color.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Theme tokens</h2>
    <p>
      The tag uses <code>--sprout-radius-control</code> and <code
        >--sprout-shadow-control</code
      >. Motion uses <code>--sprout-motion-normal</code> and <code
        >--sprout-motion-ease</code
      >. The color comes from the <code>color</code> prop, stored in <code
        >--sprout-presence-color</code
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
